/* Section: Resume Deep Dives.
   Interview pages for the projects on Leyla's resume: the follow-up
   questions an interviewer asks after "tell me about this project".
   Pages use the same Q()/M() cards, Conceptual/Math/Coding tabs, and
   difficulty mix as sections/interview-prep.js, which exports them as
   window.INTV_KIT. Coding problems live in coding/interview.js. */
(function () {
const { Q, M, tabbed } = window.INTV_KIT;

section({
  id: "resume",
  name: "Resume Deep Dives",
  cap: "The follow-up questions behind each project on the resume, with answers to reveal",
  sections: [
    { id: "how", label: "How to Use These Pages" },
    { id: "story", label: "Telling a Project Story" }
  ],
  body: `
<h3 id="sec-how">How to Use These Pages</h3>
<p>Each page is one project from the resume. An interviewer who reads "built a memory dependence predictor" on a resume will ask for the pitch, then push on the design choices, the failure modes, how it was evaluated, and whether you know where it stops working. The questions here follow that path. They work like the other interview pages: a <strong>Conceptual</strong> tab, a <strong>Math</strong> tab, and a <strong>Coding</strong> tab, tagged Easy, Medium, or Hard, with answers behind "Show answer". The answers are written in general terms. Replace their numbers with the ones from your own model wherever you have them, since your own measurements are what makes a resume answer believable.</p>

<h3 id="sec-story">Telling a Project Story</h3>
<p>For any project, have a 60-second version ready with four beats:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Problem:</strong> what was slow or missing, and how you knew (a counter, a stall breakdown, a gap to an ideal).</li>
<li><strong>Design:</strong> the mechanism, and the one or two choices that weren't obvious, with the alternative you rejected.</li>
<li><strong>Evaluation:</strong> the baseline, the upper bound, the workloads, and the result as a number.</li>
<li><strong>Limits:</strong> where it doesn't work and what you would do next.</li>
</ol>
<p class="callout">Interviewers dig into beat 4. Knowing where your own design breaks shows you understood it, not just built it.</p>`
}, [
{
  id: "resume-mdp",
  title: "Memory Dependence Predictor",
  body: `
<p>Background: <a href="#/r/mdep">Memory Dependence Prediction</a> (the store sets paper section by section), <a href="#/lsq">Load and Store Queues</a>, <a href="#/flush-recovery">Pipeline Flush and Recovery</a>. General memory dependence questions are also on <a href="#/intv-lsq/mdp">Loads, Stores and Memory Ordering</a>. Core sizes come from <a href="#/r/intv/numbers">Numbers Worth Knowing</a>.</p>
<h3 id="sec-pitch">The Pitch</h3>` +
Q(`<p>Give the 60-second pitch for a memory dependence predictor project: what problem it solves, what you built, and why it matters more on a current core than it did on a 1990s one.</p>`,
`<p><strong>Problem:</strong> a load's address is often ready before the addresses of older stores. If it waits for all of them, it is usually waiting for nothing (a false dependence). If it goes early and one of those stores writes its bytes, it read stale data, and the core must flush the load and everything younger (a memory-order violation).</p>
<p><strong>What I built:</strong> a predictor that learns from violations which stores each load depends on, and makes the load wait only for those, so most loads run early and the few dependent ones wait for exactly the right store. Store sets do this with two small tables, the SSIT (PC to set ID) and the LFST (set ID to the last fetched store of that set).</p>
<p><strong>Why it matters more now:</strong> a 1990s core had a few dozen instructions in flight, so few store-load pairs were in the window together and a flush was cheap. A Golden Cove-class core has a 512-entry ROB and up to 114 stores in flight. More dependences fall inside the window, a load sees many more unresolved older stores, and each flush throws away hundreds of instructions. Both naive speculation and "wait for every store" get worse as the window grows, which is the gap a predictor closes.</p>`, "easy") +
Q(`<p>Define a memory-order violation and a false dependence. Which one does "no speculation" produce, which one does "naive speculation" produce, and what does a perfect predictor produce?</p>`,
`<ul><li><strong>Memory-order violation:</strong> a load executes before an older store to overlapping bytes and reads a stale value. Detected when the store executes and searches the load queue, and fixed by squashing the load and everything younger.</li><li><strong>False dependence:</strong> a load waits for a store whose address turns out not to overlap its own.</li></ul>
<p>No speculation (every load waits for all older stores) produces only false dependences. Naive speculation (every load goes as soon as its address is ready) produces only violations. A perfect predictor produces neither, since each load waits for exactly the store it depends on. These are the two bounds every predictor result should be placed between.</p>`, "easy") +
M(`<p>An AMD Zen 4-class core runs a workload at 0.55 cycles/instruction with perfect memory dependence prediction. Under naive speculation it sees 1.8 memory-order violations per 1,000 instructions, and each costs 22 cycles on average.</p><ol><li>How much slower is naive speculation than perfect?</li><li>A predictor adds false dependences instead, each delaying the critical path by 6 cycles on average. Above how many false dependences per 1,000 instructions does that predictor lose to naive speculation?</li></ol><p class="given">Assume penalties add to the CPI with no overlap and the predictor causes no violations.</p>`,
`<ol><li>Violation cost: (1.8 violations / 1,000 instructions) × 22 cycles/violation = 0.0396 cycles/instruction. CPI = 0.55 cycles/instruction + 0.0396 cycles/instruction = 0.5896 cycles/instruction, and 0.5896 / 0.55 = 1.072, so naive speculation is <strong>7.2% slower</strong>.</li><li>Break-even: (x false dependences / 1,000 instructions) × 6 cycles/false dependence = 0.0396 cycles/instruction, so x = 0.0396 cycles/instruction × 1,000 instructions / 6 cycles = <strong>6.6 false dependences per 1,000 instructions</strong>.</li></ol>
<p>The point for an interview: a predictor that removes every violation can still lose if it adds more than about 6.6 critical false dependences per 1,000 instructions. Report both counts, and IPC, never just the violation rate.</p>`, "medium") +
`<h3 id="sec-design">Design Choices</h3>` +
Q(`<p>In store sets, why do the stores within one set execute in program order? What would it cost to let a load wait for several unordered stores instead?</p>`,
`<p>A load can depend on several stores, for example stores on the two sides of an if/else, or several stores filling fields of one word. To wait for all of them directly, the load would need one dependence tag per store, and the scheduler would have to track several memory tags per load. It would also have to know which store finishes last, which hardware can't know at fetch.</p>
<p>Chaining the stores solves this with one tag each. Each store waits for the previous fetched store of its set, and the load waits for the last one. The LFST holds just that last store's inum. Waiting on the end of the chain implies waiting for all of them.</p>
<p>Two side benefits come with the chain:</p>
<ul><li>Two stores to the same address in one set can't reorder, so the core needs no write-after-write check to know the load wants the second one.</li><li>Stores on different paths never wait on each other, since only the store actually fetched updates the LFST.</li></ul>
<p>The cost is that unrelated stores in one set (after merging or aliasing) are serialized, which can delay a store and every load behind it. In the paper this cost was small.</p>`, "medium") +
Q(`<p>The SSIT is indexed by PC and has no tags. What problem does that cause, what does the design do about it, and how would you decide whether tags are worth adding?</p>`,
`<p><strong>Problem:</strong> any load or store whose PC maps to a valid SSIT entry uses that entry's SSID, even if it never conflicted with anything. That instruction then joins a store set by accident and picks up false dependences. Over time, untagged entries also go stale, so the whole table drifts toward imposing random ordering.</p>
<p><strong>What the design does:</strong> it makes the SSIT large (4K entries was enough in the paper, while 256 entries lost up to about 40% IPC on the worst program) and clears all valid bits periodically, for example every million cycles, so stale and aliased entries die and real dependences are relearned with one violation each.</p>
<p><strong>Deciding on tags:</strong> sweep SSIT size with and without partial tags, and measure false dependences per 1,000 instructions plus IPC. Tags pay off if a tagged table with fewer entries matches an untagged larger table in IPC at less area. Remember that a tag array also adds a compare to the fetch-stage lookup path. Pay attention to large-footprint code (databases, browsers, JITs), where many PCs compete for entries and aliasing is worst.</p>`, "medium") +
M(`<p>You are sizing a store set predictor for a Golden Cove-class core: 512-entry ROB (an inum is the ROB index), 6-wide rename. The SSIT has 4,096 entries and the LFST has 256 entries. Every entry has a valid bit.</p><ol><li>How many bytes do the SSIT and LFST hold?</li><li>In the worst case, how many SSIT reads, LFST reads, and LFST writes per cycle must the front end support?</li><li>Cyclic clearing sweeps the SSIT valid bits 64 bits per cycle. How many cycles does a full clear take?</li></ol><p class="given">Assume any of the 6 instructions renamed in a cycle can be a load or a store, and each store both reads and writes the LFST.</p>`,
`<ol><li>An SSID names one of 256 LFST entries: log<sub>2</sub>(256 entries) = 8 bits. An inum names one of 512 ROB entries: log<sub>2</sub>(512 entries) = 9 bits.<br>SSIT: 4,096 entries × (8 bits + 1 valid bit) = 36,864 bits = 36,864 bits / 8 bits/byte = <strong>4,608 bytes</strong>.<br>LFST: 256 entries × (9 bits + 1 valid bit) = 2,560 bits = 2,560 bits / 8 bits/byte = <strong>320 bytes</strong>.<br>Total: 4,608 bytes + 320 bytes = <strong>4,928 bytes</strong>.</li><li>Every load and store reads the SSIT, so up to <strong>6 SSIT reads per cycle</strong>. Every load and store whose SSID is valid reads the LFST, so up to <strong>6 LFST reads per cycle</strong>. Every store writes its inum, so up to <strong>6 LFST writes per cycle</strong> if all 6 are stores. Real designs limit stores per rename group or bank the tables. Two instructions of one set in the same group also need an in-group bypass: a load behind a store of its set must take that store's inum from the group, not the stale value in the LFST.</li><li>4,096 bits / 64 bits/cycle = <strong>64 cycles</strong>, negligible against a clearing interval of around a million cycles. The sweep can also run in the background a few bits at a time.</li></ol>`, "hard") +
Q(`<p>A store issues and invalidates its LFST entry if the entry still holds its inum. Why the "if it still holds its inum" check? What would go wrong without it?</p>`,
`<p>After a store is fetched, a younger store of the same set may be fetched and overwrite the LFST entry with its own inum. If the older store then issues and clears the entry unconditionally, it erases the younger store's record. A load of that set fetched next would see an invalid entry, issue without waiting, and could violate against the younger store, which has not issued yet.</p>
<p>With the check, only the store the entry actually names can clear it, so the entry always points at the last fetched, not yet issued store of the set, or is invalid when none is in flight.</p>`, "medium") +
Q(`<p>An interviewer asks: "The Alpha 21264 got by with a 1,024-entry, 1-bit wait table cleared every 16,384 cycles. Why did you need something as complex as store sets?" Make the case, and say when the wait table would be the right choice.</p>`,
`<p>A wait bit says only <em>whether</em> a load should wait, not <em>for which store</em>, so a flagged load waits for every older store with an unknown address. Three things make that worse on a big core:</p>
<ul><li><strong>More unrelated stores:</strong> with around a hundred stores in flight, a flagged load waits behind many stores it has nothing to do with. Moshovos et al. found that "wait for all older stores" for truly dependent loads can lose even to blind speculation when the producing store is far back.</li><li><strong>One conflict flags every instance:</strong> a load that conflicts once in a while waits for everything until the next clear, which turns a rare violation into a frequent false dependence.</li><li><strong>No ordering for the store side:</strong> a wait table can't make two loads wait for the one store they share while leaving other stores alone.</li></ul>
<p>Store sets cost more: about 4 KB plus 0.3 KB of tables instead of 128 bytes, an LFST lookup and update in the front end, and recovery handling. That buys waiting on exactly one store per load.</p>
<p><strong>When the wait table is right:</strong> a small window (a few dozen instructions, few stores in flight), area- or power-limited cores, or workloads where the measured gap between the wait table and an oracle is small. Make the decision with data: run the wait table, store sets, and the oracle on the target workloads at the target window size, and compare IPC and false dependences on the critical path.</p>`, "hard") +
`<h3 id="sec-failure">Failure Modes and Fixes</h3>` +
Q(`<p>Two loads depend on the same store, and each store can be in only one store set. Describe what goes wrong if a store simply joins the set of whichever load it last violated with. What rule fixes it, why does that rule converge, and what does it cost?</p>`,
`<p><strong>What goes wrong:</strong> load A violates with store S, so S moves into A's set. Load B now has no reason to wait for S, so it violates with S, and S moves into B's set. Then A violates again. The two loads take turns trapping on S forever. This is oscillation, like two hot lines thrashing one cache set, and each step costs a full flush.</p>
<p><strong>The fix (store set assignment rules):</strong> on a violation, if neither instruction has a set, allocate one. If only one has a set, the other joins it. If both have sets, a fixed arbitration picks a winner, the smaller SSID, and the instruction in the losing set moves to the winner.</p>
<p><strong>Why it converges:</strong> the arbitration always picks the same winner for the same two sets, so instructions only ever move toward the smaller SSID. SSIDs are finite, so after a bounded number of violations every instruction involved ends up in one merged set and stays there.</p>
<p><strong>The cost:</strong> merging adds false dependences, since load A now also waits for stores only B needed. Chains get longer too, because all of those stores are now ordered. The paper measured no visible cost from this, while removing merging lost 12% IPC on average. On code where many loads share a few stores, check for very large merged sets, a symptom you can count directly in the model.</p>`, "hard") +
Q(`<p>Your predictor matches perfect prediction on every benchmark but one, a loop-heavy floating-point code that runs about 2× slower than with perfect prediction, and the counters show few violations. How do you diagnose it, and what are the fixes?</p>`,
`<p><strong>Hypothesis:</strong> few violations plus a large gap points at false dependences on the critical path, not violations. The classic cause is a load and store PC pair that conflicts only for some dynamic instances, for example a nested loop where the load in one iteration reads what the store wrote a few iterations earlier, but not every iteration. Store sets know only PCs, so the load waits for the latest instance of the store every time, and the loop is serialized.</p>
<p><strong>Diagnosis:</strong></p>
<ol><li>Rank load PCs by cycles spent waiting on a predicted store dependence.</li><li>For the top PCs, log at execute whether the waited-for store's address actually overlapped. If it rarely did, those are false dependences.</li><li>Log the dynamic distance (in iterations or instructions) between each load and the store instance it truly depends on. A fixed distance larger than 1 is the instance problem.</li><li>Confirm by running that loop with perfect prediction only for those PCs. The gap should close.</li></ol>
<p><strong>Fixes:</strong></p>
<ul><li>Compiler unrolling, so each instance pair has its own PCs (in the store sets paper, unrolling applu matched perfect).</li><li>A predictor that records dependence distance, as Moshovos et al. did, so the load waits for the store N instances back instead of the latest.</li><li>Confidence counters per store, to stop enforcing a dependence that rarely holds. These are vulnerable to aliasing in an untagged table, so measure them on all the benchmarks, not just this one.</li></ul>`, "hard") +
M(`<p>On a Zen 4-class core, a loop runs <code>for (i = 4; i &lt; n; i++) a[i] = a[i-4] + b[i];</code> for n = 1,000,000 iterations. Store-to-load forwarding takes 5 cycles and the add takes 1 cycle, so each link from one store to the next store that depends on it is 6 cycles. Once the store has joined the load's store set, find the run time in cycles under store sets and under perfect prediction, and the speedup from unrolling the loop by 4.</p><p class="given">Assume the dependence chains are the only limit: width, ports, and the b[i] loads never bind, and the loop starts with an empty pipeline.</p>`,
`<ol><li>True dependences: iteration i reads a[i-4], written 4 iterations earlier, so there are 4 independent chains (i mod 4 = 0, 1, 2, 3), each advancing one iteration per 6 cycles.</li><li>Perfect prediction: throughput = 4 chains × 1 iteration / 6 cycles = 0.667 iterations/cycle, so run time = 1,000,000 iterations / (4 chains × 1 iteration / 6 cycles) = <strong>1,500,000 cycles</strong>.</li><li>Store sets: the load PC and store PC share one SSID, and each load waits for the last fetched store of its set, which is iteration i-1's store. Each iteration now waits for the previous one, so there is 1 chain: run time = 1,000,000 iterations × 6 cycles/iteration = <strong>6,000,000 cycles</strong>, 4× slower than perfect.</li><li>Unrolled by 4: each copy of the body has its own load PC and store PC. The load in copy k depends only on the store in copy k, so each pair forms its own set and the 4 chains run in parallel again: about <strong>1,500,000 cycles</strong>, a 6,000,000 cycles / 1,500,000 cycles = <strong>4× speedup</strong>.</li></ol>`, "hard") +
Q(`<p>After a branch mispredict, the LFST may still name stores that were just squashed. Walk through what can go wrong if recovery does nothing to the LFST, assuming an inum is a ROB index.</p>`,
`<p>A load fetched after recovery reads the LFST and gets the inum of a squashed store. Two things can happen:</p>
<ol><li><strong>The ROB slot is still empty:</strong> the load waits for a "store issued" event that will never come. If nothing else wakes it, the load never issues and the core deadlocks behind it at retirement.</li><li><strong>The ROB slot has been reused:</strong> the new instruction in that slot may be anything, possibly younger than the load. If the scheduler treats its issue as the wakeup, the load waits on an unrelated instruction, which costs time. If that instruction is younger and depends on the load, the two wait on each other and the core deadlocks.</li></ol>
<p>Fixes, in order of cost:</p>
<ul><li>Invalidate every LFST entry that names a squashed inum (compare each entry's inum against the squash point, which is cheap for a 128 to 256 entry table).</li><li>Checkpoint the LFST at each branch with the rest of the rename state.</li><li>Use inums with a wrap bit, so the scheduler can detect a dependence on something younger than the load and ignore it.</li></ul>
<p>The SSIT needs no recovery, because it holds learned history, not in-flight state. This is a good resume answer because it shows you thought about the predictor's correctness and liveness, not just its accuracy.</p>`, "hard") +
`<h3 id="sec-eval">Evaluation and Validation</h3>` +
Q(`<p>Your predictor shows zero memory-order violations on a benchmark. Is that a good result? What else must you report before anyone should believe it helps?</p>`,
`<p>Not by itself. "Never speculate" also has zero violations. Zero violations says the predictor is at least as conservative as needed, not that it is useful. Also report:</p>
<ul><li>False dependences per 1,000 instructions, and ideally how many of them were on the critical path (cycles a load waited where the waited-for store didn't overlap).</li><li>IPC against both bounds: naive speculation, no speculation, and a perfect (oracle) predictor.</li><li>The breakdown of dynamic loads: not predicted, correctly predicted dependent, falsely dependent, violated.</li><li>Sensitivity to table sizes and clearing interval, and the benchmarks where it is furthest from perfect.</li></ul>
<p>A strong summary sounds like: "X% of the gap between naive speculation and perfect was closed, with Y false dependences per 1,000 instructions, and the worst case was benchmark Z because of W."</p>`, "medium") +
M(`<p>Two predictor designs were run on the same workload, on a core where a violation costs 25 cycles and 35% of false dependences land on the critical path, delaying it by 7 cycles each:</p><ul><li><strong>Design A:</strong> per 1,000 instructions, 21 loads correctly predicted dependent, 9 false dependences, 0.4 violations</li><li><strong>Design B:</strong> per 1,000 instructions, 20 loads correctly predicted dependent, 3 false dependences, 1.1 violations</li></ul><ol><li>For each design, compute precision (the fraction of predicted dependences that were real) and coverage (the fraction of real dependences that were predicted).</li><li>Compute each design's added cycles per 1,000 instructions. Which is faster?</li></ol><p class="given">Assume every real dependence either was predicted or caused a violation, and penalties add with no overlap.</p>`,
`<ol><li>Design A: precision = 21 / (21 + 9) = 21 / 30 = <strong>70.0%</strong>. Coverage = 21 / (21 + 0.4) = 21 / 21.4 = <strong>98.1%</strong>.<br>Design B: precision = 20 / (20 + 3) = 20 / 23 = <strong>87.0%</strong>. Coverage = 20 / (20 + 1.1) = 20 / 21.1 = <strong>94.8%</strong>.</li><li>Design A: 9 false dependences × 0.35 × 7 cycles + 0.4 violations × 25 cycles = 22.05 cycles + 10 cycles = <strong>32.05 cycles per 1,000 instructions</strong>.<br>Design B: 3 false dependences × 0.35 × 7 cycles + 1.1 violations × 25 cycles = 7.35 cycles + 27.5 cycles = <strong>34.85 cycles per 1,000 instructions</strong>.<br><strong>Design A is faster</strong>, by 34.85 cycles − 32.05 cycles = 2.8 cycles per 1,000 instructions.</li></ol>
<p>B looks better on precision, but a violation costs about 10× as much as a critical false dependence here (25 cycles vs. 0.35 × 7 cycles = 2.45 cycles), so coverage matters more. The interview point is that neither accuracy number decides it. Weight each kind of error by its cost.</p>`, "hard") +
Q(`<p>How did you make sure the predictor model is <em>correct</em>, as opposed to just producing good numbers? Name invariants you would assert and tests you would write.</p>`,
`<p><strong>Invariants to assert every cycle or event:</strong></p>
<ul><li>Architectural results never depend on the predictor: run the same trace with the predictor on, off, and replaced by random predictions, and check that committed loads return identical values. Only timing may change.</li><li>An LFST entry, if valid, names an in-flight, not yet issued store whose SSIT entry holds that same SSID.</li><li>No load waits on an inum younger than itself, or on a squashed instruction.</li><li>Every waiting load eventually issues: a watchdog flags any load that waits more than some bound, such as the ROB size times the maximum latency, which catches the recovery deadlocks.</li><li>Violation count under the oracle predictor is zero, and false-dependence count under the oracle is zero.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Directed tests:</strong></p>
<ul><li>A single store-load pair: the first run violates, later runs don't.</li><li>Two loads sharing a store: check that the sets merge and the violations stop, instead of oscillating.</li><li>Stores on two paths of an if/else feeding one load.</li><li>A branch mispredict with a squashed store in the LFST.</li><li>A periodic clear: exactly one violation per pair to relearn.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Cross-checks:</strong> make sure the violation count with the predictor off matches an independent trace analysis of store-load distances within the window, and compare against published results, for example the near-perfect store sets numbers on SPEC95, with a matching configuration.</p>`, "hard") +
`<h3 id="sec-scaling">Scaling to Modern Cores</h3>` +
Q(`<p>Store sets were evaluated in 1998 on an 8-wide core with a 128-entry window. What changes when you put them in a core with a 512-entry ROB, 6-wide rename, 3 loads and 2 stores per cycle, and SMT?</p>`,
`<ul><li><strong>More static pairs in flight:</strong> a larger window exposes store-load pairs that are farther apart (Moshovos et al. saw the count keep climbing into windows of hundreds of instructions), so the SSIT needs more entries and the LFST more sets before aliasing and forced sharing hurt.</li><li><strong>Group lookups:</strong> 6 instructions per cycle read the SSIT and LFST, and stores write the LFST in the same cycle. A load behind a store of its own set in the same group must get that store's inum by in-group bypass, the same way rename handles register dependences within a group.</li><li><strong>Longer chains cost more:</strong> stores within a set execute in order. With more stores in flight, a big merged set can serialize many stores and delay the loads behind them. Watch merged-set sizes.</li><li><strong>Recovery:</strong> more in-flight stores per squash means more LFST entries to invalidate. Checkpointing or an inum compare must be fast.</li><li><strong>SMT:</strong> two threads share untagged tables by PC, so they alias each other. Either partition the tables or add a thread ID to the index or tag, and separate the LFST per thread, since a load must never wait for the other thread's store.</li><li><strong>Security:</strong> speculative store bypass (Spectre v4) is the violation case used as a side channel. The mitigation (SSBD on x86) makes loads wait for older store addresses, which is "no speculation" for the protected code, and its cost is exactly the false-dependence cost measured above.</li></ul>`, "hard") +
Q(`<p>A customer enables speculative store bypass disable (SSBD) for a sandboxed process. Explain the performance effect in terms of your predictor, and how you would model it.</p>`,
`<p>SSBD forbids a load from executing before older stores' addresses are known, so for that process the predictor's output is overridden by "wait for every older store address." That is the no-speculation policy: violations go to zero and every load that would have passed an unresolved store now waits. The cost is the false-dependence cost of no speculation, which grows with the number of in-flight stores with unknown addresses, so it is worst on code with many stores whose addresses come from slow loads (pointer chasing, indirect stores).</p>
<p>To model it, add a per-thread mode bit that forces every load to depend on all older stores' address generation, not their data, since SSBD waits for addresses only. Then report IPC with and without it, plus false dependences per 1,000 instructions, on the customer's workload. Expect the hit to vary widely between workloads, from almost nothing on code with few stores to large on store-heavy code with late-resolving addresses.</p>`, "medium"),
},
{
  id: "resume-banks",
  title: "Cache Bank Organization",
  body: `
<p>Background: <a href="#/cache-banking">Banking and Bank Size</a> (bank timing, conflicts, and which address bits pick the bank), <a href="#/l2-banks">L2 and Banking</a>, <a href="#/cache-replacement">Cache Replacement</a>. The companion project page is <a href="#/resume-bankpred">Bank Activation Prediction Table</a>. Core sizes come from <a href="#/r/intv/numbers">Numbers Worth Knowing</a>.</p>
<p>The answers assume a banked L1D model with these parts, so replace them with your own where they differ:</p>
<ul><li>A bank mapping function (word-interleaved, line-interleaved, or XOR-hashed).</li><li>A bank busy time in cycles.</li><li>Per-cycle conflict detection with same-line merging, and age-ordered arbitration among loads, store drains, and fills.</li><li>A replay path for a load that loses arbitration, including the dependents already woken for it.</li><li>An activation count per bank per cycle, which feeds the energy model, plus an optional low-power (drowsy) state for idle banks.</li></ul>
<h3 id="sec-pitch">The Pitch</h3>` +
Q(`<p>Give the 60-second pitch for a cache bank organization study: what question it answers, what the model captures, and what decision it fed.</p>`,
`<p><strong>Question:</strong> how should an L1D that serves 3 loads and 2 stores per cycle be split into banks, and which address bits should pick the bank, so that the core gets that bandwidth from single-ported SRAM without losing IPC to conflicts or spending too much energy?</p>
<p><strong>What the model captures:</strong> the bank each access maps to, how long a bank stays busy after an access, which accesses collide in a cycle (with two loads to the same line sharing one read), who wins arbitration, and what the loser costs: a replay of the load and of every dependent the scheduler already woke for it. It also counts bank activations, since each one swings a bank's bitlines and sets the dynamic energy, and models idle banks that drop into a low-leakage state.</p>
<p><strong>Decision it fed:</strong> the bank count, the bank-selection function (for example XOR-hashing upper address bits so page-aligned arrays stop colliding), and whether idle banks may be put to sleep. The result is a curve of IPC and energy against bank count for each mapping, with the workloads that break each choice called out.</p>`, "easy") +
Q(`<p>Define a bank activation and a bank conflict. Why does your model count activations separately from accesses?</p>`,
`<ul><li><strong>Bank activation:</strong> one bank doing one read or write: its decoder fires a wordline, its bitlines swing, and its sense amplifiers fire. It is the unit of dynamic energy in a banked array.</li><li><strong>Bank conflict:</strong> two or more accesses that need the same bank while it can serve only one of them, either in the same cycle or while the bank is still busy from an earlier access. One wins and the rest wait or replay.</li></ul>
<p>Activations and accesses differ in both directions, so counting accesses gets energy wrong:</p>
<ul><li>Two loads to the same line in the same cycle are 2 accesses but 1 activation, because one bank read serves both.</li><li>A 32 B vector load in a cache with 8 B banks is 1 access but 4 activations.</li><li>A 64 B fill in the same cache is 1 access but 8 activations, which also blocks 8 banks for loads that cycle.</li></ul>`, "easy") +
`<h3 id="sec-activations">Bank Activations</h3>` +
Q(`<p>Compare word-interleaved banking (16 banks of 8 B, bank = address bits 6–3) with line-interleaved banking (16 banks, bank = address bits 9–6) for an L1D. For each, how many banks do these activate: an 8 B scalar load, a 32 B vector load, a 64 B line fill, and an 8 B store drain? Which one would you pick for an L1D, and why?</p><p class="given">Assume all accesses are naturally aligned and the cache reads every way in parallel with the tags.</p>`,
`<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Access</th><th>Word-Interleaved</th><th>Line-Interleaved</th></tr>
<tr><td>8 B scalar load</td><td>1 bank</td><td>1 bank</td></tr>
<tr><td>32 B vector load</td><td>4 banks</td><td>1 bank</td></tr>
<tr><td>64 B fill</td><td>8 banks</td><td>1 bank</td></tr>
<tr><td>8 B store drain</td><td>1 bank</td><td>1 bank</td></tr></table></div>
<p>The counts don't settle it, because the activations are not the same size. A line-interleaved bank must deliver any word of any way's line, so each activation senses a row that holds whole lines and the column mux throws most of it away. A word-interleaved bank holds only one word column, so each activation senses far fewer bits. Energy per load is closer than the table suggests.</p>
<p>For an L1D, word interleaving usually wins on bandwidth: neighboring scalar loads land in different banks or merge, while line interleaving puts two loads to the same line in the same bank (free only with merging) and puts every load of a stream into one bank for the whole line. Its costs are the fill, which blocks half the banks for a cycle, and wide vector loads, which take several banks each. Line interleaving is the better fit for an L2 or LLC, whose requests are whole lines.</p>`, "medium") +
Q(`<p>Your model shows that 16 banks use about half the L1D dynamic energy of 4 banks, at the same IPC. A reviewer says the comparison is unfair to the 4-bank design. What did the energy model probably leave out?</p>`,
`<p>A model that charges only for bitline and sense-amplifier switching in the activated bank will always favor more, smaller banks. What grows with the bank count is missing:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Crossbar and routing:</strong> every load port must reach every bank, so the crossbar grows with ports × banks, and its wires get longer as the array spreads out. That energy is spent on every access.</li><li><strong>Per-bank peripherals:</strong> each bank has its own decoders, sense amplifiers, precharge, and drivers. Their area and leakage grow with the bank count even when the banks are idle.</li><li><strong>Conflict logic:</strong> bank and line comparators for every pair of ports, plus arbitration, every cycle.</li><li><strong>Latency:</strong> a bigger crossbar can add a gate level or a wire stage, and if it costs a cycle of load-to-use latency, the "same IPC" result no longer holds.</li></ul>
<p>The fix is to take per-access energy, leakage, and area for each organization from a circuit model such as CACTI (which models banks and the routing between them), or from the SRAM team, and to report total L1D energy per instruction, not energy per activation.</p>`, "medium") +
Q(`<p>To cut leakage, idle L1D banks drop into a drowsy state after some idle time, and a bank takes 1 cycle to wake. Walk through what happens in an out-of-order pipeline when a load finds its bank drowsy. Why is this much harder to make pay off in an L1D than in an L1I or L2?</p>`,
`<p><strong>In the pipeline:</strong> the scheduler woke the load's dependents assuming the normal hit latency, for example 5 cycles. The load reaches its bank, finds it drowsy, and its data arrives a cycle late. The dependents that issued expecting the data on time must be cancelled and reissued, the same replay as a bank conflict. So one wake cycle costs the load's delay, the wasted issue slots of its dependents, and their reissue, and if the load is on the critical path the whole program loses that time.</p>
<p><strong>Why the L1D is the hard case:</strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>Latency-critical:</strong> load-to-use latency is on the critical path of most code, and an extra cycle costs a whole core cycle of energy, far more than the leakage of one bank for a few hundred cycles.</li><li><strong>Hard to predict:</strong> data addresses spread across all banks with little order, so it is hard to know which bank to wake early. An L1I fetches sequentially, so the next sub-bank is easy to predict and wake ahead, which is how Kim, Flautner, Blaauw, and Mudge (MICRO 2002) made a drowsy instruction cache work with sub-bank prediction.</li><li><strong>Short idle gaps:</strong> with 3 loads per cycle over 16 banks, most gaps between accesses to a bank are a few cycles, shorter than the break-even interval, so there is little leakage to save.</li><li><strong>The L2 tolerates it:</strong> an extra cycle on a 15-cycle L2 hit is about 7%, mostly hidden by out-of-order execution, and the L2's much larger arrays leak far more, so drowsy and gated banks are common there.</li></ul>`, "hard") +
`<h3 id="sec-conflicts">Bank Conflicts</h3>` +
Q(`<p>Why does the conflict check compare line addresses as well as bank bits? What does that comparison cost in hardware for 3 load ports, and what goes wrong in a line-interleaved L1D without it?</p>`,
`<p>Two loads to the same line in the same cycle can be served by one bank read: the bank senses the row once and the column mux hands each port its own word. Treating them as a conflict would serialize the most common pair of loads, neighboring fields of a struct or neighboring elements of an array.</p>
<p><strong>Cost:</strong> for each pair of ports, one comparator on the bank bits and one on the line address (or on the row within the bank, which is shorter). 3 ports form 3 pairs, so 3 bank comparators and 3 line comparators per cycle, plus a way for the column mux to steer one row to two ports. The line address is only known after address generation, so the compare sits on the path between the AGU and the bank decoder, which is timing-critical.</p>
<p><strong>Without it, in a line-interleaved L1D:</strong> every load in a line maps to the same bank, so two loads from one sequential stream in the same cycle always conflict. The cache would get about one load per cycle on streaming code no matter how many ports it had.</p>`, "medium") +
Q(`<p>A loop computes <code>c[i] = a[i] + b[i]</code> over arrays of doubles that each start on a 4 KB page boundary, on a core with 2 load ports and 1 store port. The L1D has 16 banks of 8 B (bank = address bits 6–3) with same-line merging. Which accesses collide, how often, and what are the fixes in hardware and software?</p><p class="given">Assume the two loads of each iteration issue in the same cycle, and stores drain from the store buffer into the L1D after they commit.</p>`,
`<p><strong>Loads:</strong> <code>a[i]</code> and <code>b[i]</code> are at the same offset from page-aligned bases, so their bits 6–3 are equal and they map to the same bank in every iteration. They are in different lines, so merging doesn't help. Every pair conflicts: one load replays, and the loop gets about 1 load per cycle instead of 2.</p>
<p><strong>Store drains:</strong> <code>c[i]</code> has the same bank bits too. A store drains several cycles after the loads of its iteration, when the loads of a later iteration are in flight, so it competes with them only when it lands on their bank at that moment. With all three arrays walking the banks in lockstep, the drain of <code>c[i]</code> lines up with the loads of <code>a[i+k]</code> and <code>b[i+k]</code> only when k is a multiple of 16 elements, so it collides some of the time, depending on how far behind the drain runs.</p>
<p><strong>Fixes:</strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>Hardware:</strong> XOR upper address bits into the bank number (for example bits 6–3 XOR bits 15–12). The arrays sit on different pages, so they differ above bit 12 and usually land in different banks. Real cores also remove the conflict by giving each load port its own copy of the data or more banks than ports need.</li><li><strong>Software:</strong> offset one array by 8 B or more (for example allocate <code>b</code> with 64 B of padding), so its bank bits differ from <code>a</code>'s. This is the same fix as padding a shared-memory array on a GPU.</li></ul>`, "hard") +
Q(`<p>Loads, store-buffer drains, and line fills all compete for L1D banks. Your first model always let loads win. What went wrong, and what arbitration would you use instead?</p>`,
`<p><strong>What goes wrong with loads always winning:</strong> on load-heavy code the other two starve.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Fills starve:</strong> a fill that can't write holds its MSHR and fill buffer. With all MSHRs held, new misses can't start, so memory-level parallelism collapses, and the loads that "won" end up waiting longer for their own misses.</li><li><strong>Stores starve:</strong> committed stores can't drain, so the store buffer fills, and allocation stalls on the next store. The front of the machine stops, which costs far more than a delayed load. A store-heavy loop competing with its own loads hits this.</li><li><strong>Deadlock risk:</strong> a load can be waiting on the very fill or store (through forwarding limits or a memory fence) that the arbiter keeps rejecting.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Better arbitration:</strong> loads have priority by default because they are on the critical path, with escalation:</p>
<ul><li>A fill or drain that has waited more than N cycles, or a store buffer or fill buffer past a high-water mark, gets priority for the next cycle.</li><li>Fills can be split to write a few banks per cycle, so a fill never blocks every bank at once.</li><li>The model should report cycles each requester lost to arbitration, and the store-buffer-full and MSHR-full stalls that result, so the trade-off can be tuned against IPC.</li></ul>`, "hard") +
`<h3 id="sec-stalls">Pipeline Stalls</h3>` +
Q(`<p>Walk through the cycles after a load loses a bank conflict on an out-of-order core with speculative wakeup and a 5-cycle load-to-use latency. What does the core throw away, and what are the ways to reduce the cost?</p>`,
`<p><strong>Timeline:</strong></p>
<ol><li>Cycle 0: the scheduler issues the load and, assuming a hit, schedules its dependents to issue so they meet the data in cycle 5.</li><li>Cycles 1 and 2: the address is generated and the bank is arbitrated. The load loses, but the scheduler has already moved on.</li><li>Cycles 3 to 5: dependents issue on schedule, and their own dependents may be woken in turn.</li><li>When the conflict signal reaches the scheduler, every µop that issued on the speculative wakeup is cancelled and returned to the scheduler, and the load is re-sent.</li></ol>
<p><strong>What it throws away:</strong> the issue slots and execution-unit cycles of every cancelled dependent, plus the delay of the load itself, which reaches the program's run time only if the load is on the critical path. One conflict can waste several issue slots, which matters only when issue bandwidth is short.</p>
<p><strong>Reducing the cost:</strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>Fewer conflicts:</strong> more banks, merging, XOR hashing, or ports with their own copies.</li><li><strong>Avoid them at issue:</strong> the scheduler doesn't know addresses yet, so it can use a bank predictor to avoid picking two loads predicted to the same bank in one cycle (Yoaz and colleagues, ISCA 1999). This is the companion project.</li><li><strong>Cheaper recovery:</strong> selective replay of just the dependence chain instead of everything issued in the window, or delaying the wakeup of dependents of loads predicted to conflict.</li></ul>`, "hard") +
Q(`<p>Your model charges a fixed 1-cycle penalty per bank conflict. Explain why that can be too pessimistic and why it can be too optimistic, and what to model instead.</p>`,
`<p><strong>Too pessimistic:</strong> an out-of-order core hides a one-cycle delay on any load that is not on the critical path. The losing load often just takes a free port the next cycle and its consumers had other work to cover the gap, so the real cost is often zero.</p>
<p><strong>Too optimistic:</strong></p>
<ul><li>With speculative wakeup, the cost is a replay, not a cycle: the load comes back several cycles later and its already-issued dependents are cancelled and reissued.</li><li>With a 2-cycle bank busy time, the loser waits 2 cycles, and conflicts cascade when a stream keeps hitting the same banks.</li><li>A conflict on a load in a pointer chain or a loop-carried dependence costs the full delay, every iteration.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Model instead:</strong> the actual mechanism. Delay the load until its bank is free, cancel and reissue the µops that woke on the wrong timing, and let the out-of-order core decide what is hidden. Then report both the conflict count and the IPC change, since the ratio between them tells you how much the core hides.</p>`, "medium") +
`<h3 id="sec-eval">Evaluation and Validation</h3>` +
Q(`<p>How would you validate your bank conflict model against real hardware, before trusting it to compare organizations that don't exist yet?</p>`,
`<p><strong>Microbenchmarks with controlled addresses:</strong> an unrolled loop of independent loads from two pointers, with the offset between them swept from 0 B to a few KB in 8 B steps. Measure loads per cycle at each offset. The offsets where throughput drops show which address bits pick the bank, the bank count, whether same-line loads merge, and whether there is an XOR hash. Repeat with a store stream and with misses to see how drains and fills compete with loads.</p>
<p><strong>Counters:</strong> some cores count conflicts directly. Sandy Bridge, for example, had a bank-conflict cycles event (<code>L1D_BLOCKS.BANK_CONFLICT_CYCLES</code>). Otherwise compare replay or dispatch-cancel counters, and load-port utilization.</p>
<p><strong>Then compare the model:</strong> run the same microbenchmarks in the model configured like that core. The throughput-versus-offset curve should match in shape and position, not just on average. Then check a few real workloads for conflict count and IPC within a tolerance.</p>
<p><strong>Limits to state:</strong> validation on an existing core checks the mechanism and the replay cost, not the new organizations. For those, rely on the mechanism being right and on sensitivity sweeps, and say so.</p>`, "hard") +
Q(`<p>What invariants and directed tests would you put in the bank model to make sure it is correct, as opposed to producing plausible numbers?</p>`,
`<p><strong>Invariants, checked every cycle:</strong></p>
<ul><li>No bank starts accesses to two different lines in one cycle, and no bank starts an access while busy from an earlier one.</li><li>Every request is eventually granted: a watchdog flags any load, drain, or fill that waits more than a bound, which catches starvation from the arbitration policy.</li><li>Activations per cycle never exceed the number of banks, and each granted access adds exactly the activations its size and mapping imply, with merged loads adding none.</li><li>A replayed load is counted once as a load and once per replay, and its activation is counted only for the attempt that actually reads the bank.</li><li>Architectural results are identical with banking on or off. Banking changes timing only.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Directed tests with hand-computed answers:</strong></p>
<ul><li>Two loads to the same line: no conflict. Two to the same bank and different lines: exactly one conflict.</li><li>A stride equal to the bank span: one load per cycle, or one every busy time.</li><li>A 1-bank configuration: the model must reduce to a single-ported cache.</li><li>An infinite-bank configuration: zero conflicts, giving the upper bound for every IPC result.</li></ul>`, "hard") +
M(`<p>A core issues 3 loads per cycle to an L1D with 8 banks. Each load goes to a uniformly random bank, independently of the others, and the 3 loads are always in different lines.</p><ol><li>What is the probability that at least two of the 3 loads conflict in a cycle?</li><li>If each bank serves one load per cycle, what is the expected number of loads served in the cycle they issue?</li><li>Repeat both for 16 banks.</li></ol><p class="given">Assume the 3 loads issue every cycle and the loads that lose do not compete in that cycle again.</p>`,
`<ol><li>The loads are conflict-free only if all three banks differ: P(no conflict) = (8 banks × 7 banks × 6 banks) / (8 banks)<sup>3</sup> = 336 / 512 = 0.65625. So P(conflict) = 1 − 0.65625 = <strong>34.4%</strong>.</li><li>Loads served = distinct banks used. A given bank is unused with probability (1 − 1/8)<sup>3</sup>, so expected distinct banks = 8 banks × (1 − (7/8)<sup>3</sup>) = 8 banks × (1 − 343/512) = 8 banks × 169/512 = <strong>2.64 loads per cycle</strong>, losing 3 loads − 2.640625 loads = 0.36 loads per cycle (12.0%).</li><li>16 banks: P(no conflict) = (16 banks × 15 banks × 14 banks) / (16 banks)<sup>3</sup> = 3,360 / 4,096 = 0.8203125, so P(conflict) = <strong>18.0%</strong>. Expected distinct banks = 16 banks × (1 − (15/16)<sup>3</sup>) = 16 banks × (1 − 3,375/4,096) = 16 banks × 721/4,096 = <strong>2.82 loads per cycle</strong>, losing 3 loads − 2.81640625 loads = 0.18 loads per cycle (6.1%).</li></ol>
<p>Doubling the banks roughly halves the random conflicts. The random case is the gentle one, though: real code with aligned arrays conflicts systematically, which more banks don't fix but XOR hashing does.</p>`, "medium") +
M(`<p>A workload on a Golden Cove-class core runs at 0.42 cycles/instruction with no bank conflicts. It executes 380 loads per 1,000 instructions, and 4.2% of loads lose a bank conflict. A losing load is replayed and completes 3 cycles later than it would have, and by the time the loss is detected, 1.5 dependent µops on average have issued and must be cancelled and reissued. 30% of the losing loads are on the critical path, where the full delay reaches the run time, and the rest are hidden.</p><ol><li>How many issue slots per 1,000 instructions are wasted, and what fraction of the core's 6 issue slots per cycle is that?</li><li>How much slower is the workload than with no conflicts?</li><li>What conflict rate would hold the slowdown to 1%?</li></ol><p class="given">Assume 1 µop per instruction, the wasted issue slots never delay other work, and the critical-path delays add with no overlap.</p>`,
`<ol><li>Conflicts: 380 loads × 0.042 = 15.96 conflicts per 1,000 instructions. Each wastes the load's slot and its dependents' slots: 15.96 conflicts × (1 load + 1.5 dependent µops) = <strong>39.9 wasted issue slots per 1,000 instructions</strong>.<br>Slots available: 6 slots/cycle × 0.42 cycles/instruction × 1,000 instructions = 2,520 slots, so 39.9 slots / 2,520 slots = <strong>1.6%</strong> of issue bandwidth, small enough not to bind unless the ports are already saturated.</li><li>Critical-path delay: 15.96 conflicts × 0.30 × 3 cycles = 14.364 cycles per 1,000 instructions. CPI = 0.42 cycles/instruction + 14.364 cycles / 1,000 instructions = 0.434364 cycles/instruction, and 0.434364 / 0.42 = 1.0342, so the workload is <strong>3.4% slower</strong>.</li><li>A 1% slowdown allows 0.01 × 0.42 cycles/instruction × 1,000 instructions = 4.2 cycles per 1,000 instructions. So 380 loads × p × 0.30 × 3 cycles = 4.2 cycles, giving p = 4.2 cycles / (380 loads × 0.30 × 3 cycles) = 4.2 / 342 = <strong>1.23% of loads</strong>.</li></ol>
<p>The interview point: the 30% critical-path fraction decides everything. Measure it in the model (for example by comparing against a run where conflicting loads are not delayed) instead of assuming every conflict costs a cycle.</p>`, "hard") +
M(`<p>An L1D has 16 banks of 8 B (bank = address bits 6–3), 2 load ports, and same-line merging. A loop issues 8 B loads at a fixed stride, each to a different line. Loads issue in program order, at most 2 per cycle, and a load issues in the first cycle in which its bank is free. A bank is busy for the busy time starting at the cycle an access starts.</p><ol><li>Find the steady-state loads per cycle for strides of 64 B, 96 B, 128 B, and 136 B, with a bank busy time of 1 cycle and of 2 cycles.</li><li>The bank function is changed to bits 6–3 XOR bits 10–7. What happens to the 64 B and 128 B strides with a 2-cycle busy time?</li></ol>`,
`<ol><li>Load i is at i × stride, so its bank is (i × stride / 8 B) mod 16 banks. Let g = gcd(stride / 8 B, 16 banks). The stream then cycles through 16 / g distinct banks.
<ul><li>64 B: stride / 8 B = 8 words, g = 8, banks 0, 8, 0, 8, ... (2 banks).</li><li>96 B: stride / 8 B = 12 words, g = 4, banks 0, 12, 8, 4, 0, ... (4 banks).</li><li>128 B: stride / 8 B = 16 words, g = 16, bank 0 every time (1 bank).</li><li>136 B: stride / 8 B = 17 words, g = 1, banks 0, 1, 2, ..., 15 (16 banks).</li></ul>
With a 1-cycle busy time, a pair conflicts only if its two loads share a bank, which happens only with 1 bank. With a 2-cycle busy time, a pair also can't reuse a bank used by the previous pair, so 4 consecutive loads need 4 distinct banks.
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Stride</th><th>Distinct Banks</th><th>Busy Time 1 Cycle</th><th>Busy Time 2 Cycles</th></tr>
<tr><td>64 B</td><td>2</td><td>2 loads/cycle</td><td>1 load/cycle (each pair waits for the previous pair's banks)</td></tr>
<tr><td>96 B</td><td>4</td><td>2 loads/cycle</td><td>2 loads/cycle</td></tr>
<tr><td>128 B</td><td>1</td><td>1 load/cycle</td><td>0.5 loads/cycle</td></tr>
<tr><td>136 B</td><td>16</td><td>2 loads/cycle</td><td>2 loads/cycle</td></tr></table></div>
For 64 B with a 2-cycle busy time: cycle 0 issues loads 0 and 1 to banks 0 and 8. Load 2 needs bank 0, busy through cycle 1, so loads 2 and 3 issue in cycle 2, which is 2 loads / 2 cycles = 1 load/cycle. For 128 B, every load waits a full busy time for the one before it.</li>
<li>For 128 B, address bits 6–3 are always 0 and bits 10–7 equal i mod 16, so the bank is i mod 16: 16 distinct banks and <strong>2 loads/cycle</strong>. For 64 B, bits 6–3 are 8 × (i mod 2) and bits 10–7 are (i / 2) mod 16, so 4 consecutive loads land in banks k, 8 XOR k, k + 1, and 8 XOR (k + 1) for k = (i / 2) mod 16. These are always distinct, because k XOR (k + 1) is 1, 3, 7, or 15 and never 8, so the 64 B stride also gets <strong>2 loads/cycle</strong>.</li></ol>
<p>A 2-cycle busy time doubles the bank span that hurts: strides that use only 2 banks now lose half their throughput, not just strides that use 1. XOR hashing fixes both power-of-two cases, which is why the stride sweep belongs in any bank evaluation.</p>`, "hard") +
M(`<p>One bank of an L1D leaks 0.040 pJ/cycle awake and 0.010 pJ/cycle drowsy. Waking it costs 0.9 pJ and 1 cycle, and a bank goes drowsy after T idle cycles. Over 10,000 cycles, this bank sees these idle gaps between accesses:</p><ul><li>900 gaps of 4 cycles</li><li>40 gaps of 60 cycles</li><li>5 gaps of 400 cycles</li></ul><ol><li>What is the break-even idle time for going drowsy?</li><li>For T = 10 cycles and T = 100 cycles, how much leakage energy does the bank save, and how many wake stalls does it cause?</li><li>The core spends 1,200 pJ per cycle (6 W at 5 GHz), and 10% of wake stalls land on the critical path, each adding 1 cycle to the run time. Does either timeout save energy overall?</li></ol><p class="given">Assume the bank wakes only on demand, when the next access arrives, and ignore leakage during the wake cycle itself.</p>`,
`<ol><li>Each drowsy cycle saves 0.040 pJ/cycle − 0.010 pJ/cycle = 0.030 pJ/cycle, so break-even = 0.9 pJ / (0.040 pJ/cycle − 0.010 pJ/cycle) = <strong>30 cycles</strong> of drowsy time.</li><li>A gap of G cycles sleeps for G − T cycles and ends in one wake, if G &gt; T. The 4-cycle gaps never sleep under either timeout.
<ul><li>T = 10 cycles: each 60-cycle gap saves (60 cycles − 10 cycles) × 0.030 pJ/cycle − 0.9 pJ = 0.6 pJ, and 40 gaps × 0.6 pJ = 24 pJ. Each 400-cycle gap saves (400 cycles − 10 cycles) × 0.030 pJ/cycle − 0.9 pJ = 10.8 pJ, and 5 gaps × 10.8 pJ = 54 pJ. Total <strong>78 pJ</strong> saved with 40 + 5 = <strong>45 wake stalls</strong>.</li><li>T = 100 cycles: the 60-cycle gaps never sleep. Each 400-cycle gap saves (400 cycles − 100 cycles) × 0.030 pJ/cycle − 0.9 pJ = 8.1 pJ, and 5 gaps × 8.1 pJ = <strong>40.5 pJ</strong> saved with <strong>5 wake stalls</strong>.</li></ul>
For scale, the idle leakage without drowsy mode is (900 gaps × 4 cycles + 40 gaps × 60 cycles + 5 gaps × 400 cycles) × 0.040 pJ/cycle = 8,000 cycles × 0.040 pJ/cycle = 320 pJ, so T = 10 cycles saves 78 pJ / 320 pJ = 24% of it and T = 100 cycles saves 40.5 pJ / 320 pJ = 13%.</li>
<li>T = 10 cycles: 45 wake stalls × 0.10 × 1,200 pJ = 5,400 pJ of extra core energy against 78 pJ saved, a net loss of <strong>5,322 pJ</strong>. T = 100 cycles: 5 wake stalls × 0.10 × 1,200 pJ = 600 pJ against 40.5 pJ saved, a net loss of <strong>559.5 pJ</strong>. <strong>Neither saves energy.</strong></li></ol>
<p>On an L1D, a single unhidden wake on the critical path costs more than a bank leaks in thousands of cycles. Drowsy L1D banks pay off only if almost every wake is started early, which is what a bank activation predictor is for, or if the timeout is long enough that wakes are very rare.</p>`, "hard"),
},
{
  id: "resume-bankpred",
  title: "Bank Activation Prediction Table",
  body: `
<p>Background: <a href="#/resume-banks">Cache Bank Organization</a> (the banked L1D this predictor serves), <a href="#/cache-banking/conflicts">Bank Conflicts</a>, <a href="#/dcache/way-pred">Way Prediction in the Data Cache</a> (the closest relative), and <a href="#/cache-replacement">Cache Replacement</a>, whose policies apply to the predictor's own table. Core sizes come from <a href="#/r/intv/numbers">Numbers Worth Knowing</a>.</p>
<p>The answers assume this design, so replace it with your own where it differs:</p>
<ul><li>A set-associative table indexed by a hash of the load PC, read at rename. Each entry holds a valid bit, a partial tag, a predicted bank, and a 2-bit confidence counter.</li><li>A confident hit predicts the bank. The core uses the prediction before the address is known: it wakes or precharges that bank early, and the scheduler avoids issuing two loads predicted to the same bank in one cycle.</li><li>A miss or low confidence gives no prediction, and the load pays the normal cost (an on-demand wake, or a possible conflict).</li><li>The table trains when the address generation unit produces the real bank.</li></ul>
<h3 id="sec-pitch">The Pitch</h3>` +
Q(`<p>Give the 60-second pitch for a bank activation prediction table: what problem it solves, what you built, and what you evaluated.</p>`,
`<p><strong>Problem:</strong> a banked L1D only knows which bank a load needs after the address is generated, which is too late for two useful things. A drowsy bank can't be woken in time, so the load pays a wake cycle and a replay. And the scheduler can't avoid issuing two loads to the same bank, so they conflict and one replays.</p>
<p><strong>What I built:</strong> a small PC-indexed table that predicts each load's bank at rename. A confident prediction wakes that bank early and lets the scheduler steer around predicted conflicts, and a low-confidence load falls back to the normal path. It works because many static loads hit the same bank every time: stack slots, globals, and fields at fixed offsets.</p>
<p><strong>What I evaluated:</strong> coverage and accuracy, wake stalls and conflicts avoided, the net energy including the table's own lookups, and IPC, against an oracle and no-prediction baseline. Then sensitivity to table size and replacement policy, which decide how much of the benefit survives on workloads with large code footprints.</p>`, "easy") +
Q(`<p>Every load ends in one of three outcomes: correct prediction, wrong prediction, or no prediction. What does each cost, and how do coverage and accuracy relate to them?</p>`,
`<ul><li><strong>Correct:</strong> the right bank is woken early, or the scheduler correctly avoids a conflict. This is the only outcome that gains anything.</li><li><strong>Wrong:</strong> a bank that wasn't needed is woken (wasted wake energy), the real bank still pays its wake cycle, and the scheduler may delay a load to avoid a conflict that doesn't exist. Usually worse than no prediction.</li><li><strong>No prediction:</strong> the load behaves as if there were no predictor. It costs only the table lookup.</li></ul>
<p>Coverage is the fraction of loads that get a prediction, and accuracy is the fraction of predictions that are correct. The confidence threshold trades one against the other: raising it turns some wrong predictions into no predictions, which lowers coverage but raises accuracy. Because a wrong prediction costs more than no prediction, the right threshold depends on how much more.</p>`, "easy") +
`<h3 id="sec-design">Design Choices</h3>` +
Q(`<p>Why index by PC? Which kinds of loads does a per-PC bank prediction get right, which does it get wrong, and how would you extend the entry to cover more of them?</p>`,
`<p>The bank is a few low address bits (for example bits 6–3), and for many static loads those bits are the same every time the load runs, so the PC is a good key.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Predictable:</strong> stack loads at a fixed offset from the stack pointer (as long as the stack pointer has the same alignment each time), globals, and fields of a struct reached through a pointer that is always aligned the same way.</li><li><strong>Strided:</strong> a loop over an array moves through banks in a fixed pattern. A plain "last bank" entry is always wrong on it, unless the stride is a multiple of the bank span.</li><li><strong>Unpredictable:</strong> hash lookups, pointer chasing over objects at random alignments, and indirect accesses, whose low address bits vary from one execution to the next.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Extension:</strong> store the last bank and a bank delta, like a stride prefetcher, and predict last bank + delta. A loop that moves 8 B per iteration over 8 B banks then predicts perfectly. Confidence should cover the delta too, so random loads stay unpredicted instead of being predicted wrongly.</p>`, "medium") +
Q(`<p>The table is read at rename and trained at address generation, about 8 to 12 cycles later. What problems does that gap cause for a load in a tight loop, and how do you fix them?</p>`,
`<p><strong>Several instances in flight:</strong> in a tight loop, 3 or 4 instances of the same load PC can be renamed before the first one trains. With a last-bank-plus-delta entry, all of them read the same stale last bank and predict the same bank, but each should be one delta further on.</p>
<p><strong>Fixes:</strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>Speculative update at rename:</strong> advance the entry's predicted bank by the delta each time the PC is renamed, so each instance gets its own prediction, and keep a non-speculative copy that trains at execute. On a branch mispredict, restore the speculative copy from the non-speculative one, or from a checkpoint.</li><li><strong>In-flight count:</strong> keep a count of in-flight instances per entry and predict last bank + (count + 1) × delta, decrementing the count when an instance trains or is squashed.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Timing:</strong> the table must deliver a prediction for every load in the rename group, for example 3 loads per cycle, so it needs that many read ports or banking of its own. A larger table is slower to read, and if the lookup doesn't fit in the rename stages, the prediction arrives too late to wake a bank for the earliest-issuing loads. That puts a ceiling on table size that has nothing to do with accuracy.</p>
<p><strong>Training order:</strong> loads execute out of order, so training with "last bank seen" can mix instances. Train the delta only from instances that execute in program order, or compute it from the difference between consecutive instances' addresses tagged with their sequence numbers.</p>`, "hard") +
Q(`<p>Should the table be tagged? What does an alias cost with and without tags, and how many tag bits are enough?</p>`,
`<p><strong>Untagged:</strong> two PCs that share an entry share a prediction. If both are confident in different banks, they keep retraining the entry and lower each other's confidence, which costs coverage. If one is confident and the other never trained, the second gets the first one's bank as a confident, wrong prediction. That's the expensive outcome.</p>
<p><strong>Tagged:</strong> a partial tag turns most aliases into misses, so the load gets no prediction, which is cheap. Tag bits cost storage (an 8-bit tag more than doubles an entry that otherwise holds a valid bit, a 4-bit bank, and a 2-bit counter) and a compare on the lookup path.</p>
<p><strong>How many bits:</strong> with t tag bits, a load whose own PC isn't in the set falsely matches a given entry with probability about 1 / 2<sup>t</sup>, so with 4 ways the false-hit rate is about 4 / 2<sup>t</sup>. 8 bits gives about 4 / 256 = 1.6%, and each extra bit halves it. Measure wrong predictions caused by aliasing against tag width, and stop adding bits once they no longer move IPC or energy.</p>`, "medium") +
`<h3 id="sec-size">Sensitivity to Table Size</h3>` +
Q(`<p>You sweep the table from 64 to 4,096 entries. Accuracy flattens at 256 entries, but coverage keeps rising until 1,024. Explain both curves, and say how you would pick the size.</p>`,
`<p><strong>Accuracy flattens early:</strong> accuracy is measured only over loads that get a prediction, and the confidence counters already filter out the unpredictable PCs. Once the table is large enough that aliasing rarely corrupts an entry, a bigger table adds entries but doesn't make existing ones more correct. Any remaining wrong predictions come from loads whose bank changes, which no size fixes.</p>
<p><strong>Coverage keeps rising:</strong> coverage depends on how many of the workload's predictable PCs fit. Each doubling holds colder PCs that a smaller table evicted before they could reach confidence. It stops rising when the table holds the whole predictable footprint, here about 1,024 static loads.</p>
<p><strong>Picking the size:</strong> convert the outcomes into the metric you care about (energy saved minus table energy, or IPC) and plot it against storage. Pick the knee, where the next doubling buys little per KB. Then check three things the sweep doesn't show:</p>
<ul><li>Whether the chosen size can be read in the rename stages, for every load in the group.</li><li>Large-footprint workloads (databases, browsers, JITs), whose curve keeps rising long after SPEC's flattens.</li><li>The warm-up: short simulation samples start with an empty table and understate what a large table gives in steady state.</li></ul>`, "hard") +
Q(`<p>Your size sweep on SPEC CPU says 256 entries is enough. A colleague runs a database trace and finds you need 4×. Why are the results so different, and what should you have done?</p>`,
`<p>A SPEC benchmark spends most of its time in a few hot loops, with a few hundred static loads that matter. A database, web server, or JIT-compiled runtime spreads its time over tens of thousands of static instructions, with flat profiles and long code paths that run once per request. The predictable footprint is far bigger, and much of it is reused only after long stretches of other code, so a small table evicts entries before they are used again.</p>
<p>What to do:</p>
<ul><li>Choose the size on a suite that includes server and large-footprint traces, not only SPEC.</li><li>Report the static load footprint (distinct load PCs that cover 90% of dynamic loads) for each workload, which explains the sweep at a glance.</li><li>Check that each simulation sample is long enough, or warmed up enough, to fill the table. Otherwise every size looks equally bad on the large workloads, which hides the difference.</li></ul>`, "medium") +
`<h3 id="sec-repl">Sensitivity to Replacement Policy</h3>` +
Q(`<p>Compare LRU, random, and a confidence-aware policy (evict the lowest-confidence entry first) for the predictor table. Why can plain LRU lose to random, and why might the insertion policy matter more than the eviction choice?</p>`,
`<p><strong>LRU loses on cyclic footprints:</strong> if a loop body touches more predictable PCs than a set (or the table) holds, LRU evicts each entry just before it is used again, so nothing ever hits. Random keeps some entries by chance and gets a fraction of the hits. This is the same thrashing pattern caches see on a loop just larger than the cache.</p>
<p><strong>Confidence-aware eviction:</strong> evicting low-confidence entries first protects the entries that are actually giving predictions. An entry that never reaches confidence (a hash lookup, a pointer chase) is the cheapest to lose, because it never predicted anything.</p>
<p><strong>Insertion matters more:</strong> most entries a predictor allocates are useless: PCs that run once, or whose bank is random. Each one evicts a useful entry. Options:</p>
<ul><li>Insert new entries at the LRU position (LIP-style), so a PC must hit again before it can push out an established entry.</li><li>Allocate only on a PC's second sighting, using a small filter.</li><li>Don't allocate PCs flagged as unpredictable by a separate small table.</li></ul>
<p>Each of these needs a way to adapt when the hot set changes, for example inserting at MRU once in every 32 insertions (BIP-style). Otherwise stale entries stay forever.</p>`, "hard") +
Q(`<p>Your replacement study shows LRU and random within 0.5% of each other at 1,024 entries but 6% apart at 128 entries. What does that tell you, and how would you report it?</p>`,
`<p>Replacement only matters when the table is under pressure. At 1,024 entries the predictable footprint fits, few useful entries are ever evicted, and the choice of victim barely matters. At 128 entries, entries are evicted constantly and the policy decides which ones survive.</p>
<p>How to report it:</p>
<ul><li>Show the policies at the chosen size and at a smaller, area-constrained size. Say that the policy matters only in the second case.</li><li>For random, run several seeds and report the spread, so a 0.5% difference isn't mistaken for a result.</li><li>Split the result by workload, because the averages hide the large-footprint workloads where even 1,024 entries are under pressure.</li><li>Include the replacement state's storage and update energy: true LRU on 4 ways needs 5 bits per set, tree PLRU 3 bits, and random only a shared LFSR. If random is within noise at the chosen size, it is the cheaper choice.</li></ul>`, "medium") +
`<h3 id="sec-failure">Failure Modes</h3>` +
Q(`<p>The predictor's bank depends on the data address, but the table is keyed by PC. For a word-interleaved L1D with bank = address bits 6–3, which address bits must stay the same across executions of a load for the prediction to hold? Walk through stack, global, and heap loads, and say what breaks each one.</p>`,
`<p>The bank is address bits 6–3, so the load's address modulo 128 B must stay the same, at least down to the 8 B word.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Globals:</strong> fixed address, always predictable.</li><li><strong>Stack:</strong> the address is the stack pointer plus a fixed offset, so the prediction holds if the stack pointer has the same value modulo 128 B every time the function runs. It does when the function is always called from the same call depth and path. Calls from different call sites, with different frame sizes above, shift the stack pointer by multiples of 16 B (the ABI alignment), which changes the bank. A function called from many places is the common failure.</li><li><strong>Heap:</strong> the address is the object's base plus a field offset. The prediction holds only if every object's base has the same value modulo 128 B. Allocators align objects to 16 B and pack them by size class, so objects of a 48 B class start at many different offsets modulo 128 B, and the field's bank changes from object to object. Objects of a 128 B or larger power-of-two size class are aligned the same way, so their fields are predictable.</li></ul>
<p>This is why the same PC can be predictable in one run and not in another: a different allocator, ASLR changing the stack's starting alignment within a page, or a different call mix all change the low address bits. The model's evaluation should vary these, not just the input data.</p>`, "hard") +
Q(`<p>On a hash-join workload the predictor makes the core slower than having no predictor at all. Explain how that can happen, how you would confirm it, and what you would change.</p>`,
`<p><strong>How it can happen:</strong> the probe loads in a hash join go to effectively random banks. Some PCs reach confidence from a few lucky repeats, then predict a bank that is right about 1 time in 16. Each wrong prediction wakes the wrong bank and, if the prediction is used for scheduling, holds a load back to avoid a conflict that doesn't exist, delaying the critical probe chain. These PCs also take table entries from useful ones.</p>
<p><strong>Confirm:</strong> break the cost down per PC: wrong predictions, delays from scheduling around predicted conflicts, and wasted wakes. Then rerun with the predictor disabled only for the top offending PCs. If the slowdown disappears, those PCs are the cause.</p>
<p><strong>Change:</strong></p>
<ul><li>A stricter confidence rule, for example a 3-bit counter that must be saturated to predict, with a large step down on a miss.</li><li>An accuracy monitor per epoch (for example every 100,000 loads) that turns predictions off when accuracy falls below the break-even point, like prefetcher throttling.</li><li>A sticky "unpredictable" bit per entry, so a PC that has failed stops predicting and doesn't keep re-earning confidence.</li></ul>`, "hard") +
`<h3 id="sec-eval">Evaluation and Validation</h3>` +
Q(`<p>Lay out the full evaluation for the predictor: baselines, metrics, sweeps, and the one plot you would put in the summary.</p>`,
`<p><strong>Baselines:</strong></p>
<ul><li>No prediction: wake banks on demand and let conflicts happen.</li><li>Always awake: no drowsy banks, the leakage upper bound and stall lower bound.</li><li>Oracle: a perfect predictor, which bounds what any table can do.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Metrics:</strong> coverage, accuracy, and the three outcomes per 1,000 loads, wake stalls and conflicts avoided, IPC, and net energy: leakage saved minus wake energy, wasted wakes, table lookups and updates, and the core energy of any remaining stall cycles.</p>
<p><strong>Sweeps:</strong> table size × associativity × replacement policy × confidence threshold, on a suite that includes large-footprint server traces, with enough warm-up to fill the table. Hold the rest of the core fixed and change one factor at a time around a chosen point, then check the interactions that matter (size and replacement interact strongly).</p>
<p><strong>The summary plot:</strong> net energy saved (or IPC) against table storage in bytes, one line per replacement policy, with the oracle as a horizontal line. It shows the knee, how much of the oracle's benefit the chosen size captures, and whether the policy matters at that size.</p>`, "hard") +
Q(`<p>Your predictor saves 1.8% of L1D energy. A reviewer asks whether the table itself costs more than that. How do you answer?</p>`,
`<p>Account for the table's own costs on the same footing as the savings:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Dynamic:</strong> one read per load at rename (with as many ports as loads per rename group), plus one write per training update. Updates can be skipped when nothing changes, for example when a saturated counter confirms its bank.</li><li><strong>Leakage:</strong> the table leaks every cycle. A 1,024-entry table of about 17 bits per entry is about 2 KB, about 4% of a 48 KB L1D's storage, so its leakage is not negligible next to a 1.8% saving.</li><li><strong>Area and timing:</strong> the read must fit in the rename stages.</li></ul>
<p>Then compare the net number, L1D energy saved minus table energy, and add the core energy of stall cycles avoided, which is often the larger term. If the net saving is small, a smaller table, or skipping training updates that don't change the entry, may turn it positive. Report the net, never the gross saving alone.</p>`, "medium") +
M(`<p>A bank prediction table has 1,024 entries in 4 ways, indexed by the low bits of PC / 4 B. Each entry holds a valid bit, a 10-bit partial tag, a predicted bank for a 16-bank L1D, and a 2-bit confidence counter.</p><ol><li>How many sets does it have, and how many PC bits does the index use?</li><li>How many bytes does it take with tree PLRU, with true LRU, and with random replacement driven by one shared 16-bit LFSR?</li></ol>`,
`<ol><li>1,024 entries / 4 ways = <strong>256 sets</strong>, indexed by log<sub>2</sub>(256 sets) = <strong>8 bits</strong> of PC / 4 B (PC bits 9–2).</li><li>Entry: 1 valid bit + 10 tag bits + log<sub>2</sub>(16 banks) = 4 bank bits + 2 confidence bits = 17 bits. Entries: 1,024 entries × 17 bits = 17,408 bits.
<ul><li>Tree PLRU: 4 ways − 1 = 3 bits per set, so 256 sets × 3 bits = 768 bits. Total 17,408 bits + 768 bits = 18,176 bits = 18,176 bits / 8 bits/byte = <strong>2,272 bytes</strong>.</li><li>True LRU: 4! = 24 orderings need ⌈log<sub>2</sub>(24)⌉ = 5 bits per set, so 256 sets × 5 bits = 1,280 bits. Total 17,408 bits + 1,280 bits = 18,688 bits = 18,688 bits / 8 bits/byte = <strong>2,336 bytes</strong>.</li><li>Random: 17,408 bits + 16 bits = 17,424 bits = 17,424 bits / 8 bits/byte = <strong>2,178 bytes</strong>.</li></ul></li></ol>
<p>The replacement state is 4% to 7% of the table, so if random is within noise of LRU, it saves about 2,336 bytes − 2,178 bytes = 158 bytes and the per-hit update energy of LRU.</p>`, "medium") +
M(`<p>Without a predictor, 300 of every 1,000 loads find their L1D bank drowsy and pay a 1-cycle wake stall. The predictor's outcomes over all loads are 72% correct, 6% wrong, and 22% no prediction, spread evenly over loads whether their bank is drowsy or not.</p><ul><li>A correct prediction for a load whose bank is drowsy hides the wake.</li><li>A wrong prediction wakes a bank that would otherwise have stayed drowsy, costing 0.9 pJ, and the load's own bank still wakes on demand if it is drowsy.</li><li>Each lookup costs 0.6 pJ.</li><li>15% of wake stalls land on the critical path, and each such cycle costs 1,200 pJ of core energy.</li></ul><ol><li>What is the net energy saved per 1,000 loads?</li><li>A smaller table gets 60% correct, 9% wrong, and 31% no prediction at 0.35 pJ per lookup. Which table saves more, and by how much?</li><li>Redo part 1 for a core that hides every wake stall.</li></ol><p class="given">Assume waking a bank costs the same whether it is done early or on demand, and waking a bank that is already awake costs nothing.</p>`,
`<ol><li>Without the predictor: 300 wake stalls × 0.15 × 1,200 pJ = 54,000 pJ.<br>With it, a drowsy-bank load still stalls when the prediction is wrong or missing: 300 loads × (0.06 + 0.22) = 84 wake stalls, costing 84 wake stalls × 0.15 × 1,200 pJ = 15,120 pJ. Stall energy saved: 54,000 pJ − 15,120 pJ = 38,880 pJ.<br>Costs: wrong wakes 1,000 loads × 0.06 × 0.9 pJ = 54 pJ, and lookups 1,000 loads × 0.6 pJ = 600 pJ.<br>Net: 38,880 pJ − 54 pJ − 600 pJ = <strong>38,226 pJ per 1,000 loads</strong>.</li><li>Smaller table: 300 loads × (0.09 + 0.31) = 120 wake stalls, costing 120 wake stalls × 0.15 × 1,200 pJ = 21,600 pJ, so stall energy saved is 54,000 pJ − 21,600 pJ = 32,400 pJ. Costs: 1,000 loads × 0.09 × 0.9 pJ = 81 pJ and 1,000 loads × 0.35 pJ = 350 pJ. Net: 32,400 pJ − 81 pJ − 350 pJ = 31,969 pJ.<br><strong>The larger table saves more</strong>, by 38,226 pJ − 31,969 pJ = <strong>6,257 pJ per 1,000 loads</strong>, even though its lookups cost 1.7× as much.</li><li>With every stall hidden, there is no stall energy to save, so the net is −54 pJ − 600 pJ = <strong>−654 pJ per 1,000 loads</strong>: the predictor costs energy.</li></ol>
<p>The predictor's value rests almost entirely on the critical-path fraction of wake stalls, so that is the number to measure in the model, not to assume. Table lookup energy matters only when the stalls it removes are already hidden.</p>`, "hard") +
M(`<p>For a server workload, the dynamic loads come from static load PCs ranked by how often they run. Per 1,000 loads:</p><ul><li>PCs ranked 1 to 128: 610 loads</li><li>PCs ranked 129 to 512: 180 loads</li><li>PCs ranked 513 to 2,048: 90 loads</li><li>PCs ranked above 2,048: 120 loads</li></ul><p>A load whose PC is in the table is predicted correctly 90% of the time, wrongly 4%, and not at all 6%. A load whose PC is not in the table gets no prediction. A correct prediction saves 0.05 cycles on average and a wrong one costs 0.02 cycles, and 1 cycle costs 1,200 pJ of core energy. Each lookup costs 0.15 pJ, 0.30 pJ, or 0.80 pJ for 128, 512, or 2,048 entries, and an entry is 17 bits.</p><ol><li>Find the net energy saved per 1,000 loads for 128, 512, and 2,048 entries.</li><li>Find the energy saved per extra byte of storage for each step up in size. Which size would you pick?</li></ol><p class="given">Assume replacement keeps exactly the hottest PCs that fit.</p>`,
`<ol><li>Loads covered: 128 entries cover 610 loads, 512 entries cover 610 loads + 180 loads = 790 loads, and 2,048 entries cover 790 loads + 90 loads = 880 loads.
<ul><li>128 entries: 610 loads × 0.90 = 549 correct and 610 loads × 0.04 = 24.4 wrong. Cycles saved: 549 correct × 0.05 cycles − 24.4 wrong × 0.02 cycles = 27.45 cycles − 0.488 cycles = 26.962 cycles. Net: 26.962 cycles × 1,200 pJ/cycle − 1,000 loads × 0.15 pJ = 32,354.4 pJ − 150 pJ = <strong>32,204.4 pJ</strong>.</li><li>512 entries: 790 loads × 0.90 = 711 correct and 790 loads × 0.04 = 31.6 wrong. Cycles saved: 711 correct × 0.05 cycles − 31.6 wrong × 0.02 cycles = 35.55 cycles − 0.632 cycles = 34.918 cycles. Net: 34.918 cycles × 1,200 pJ/cycle − 1,000 loads × 0.30 pJ = 41,901.6 pJ − 300 pJ = <strong>41,601.6 pJ</strong>.</li><li>2,048 entries: 880 loads × 0.90 = 792 correct and 880 loads × 0.04 = 35.2 wrong. Cycles saved: 792 correct × 0.05 cycles − 35.2 wrong × 0.02 cycles = 39.6 cycles − 0.704 cycles = 38.896 cycles. Net: 38.896 cycles × 1,200 pJ/cycle − 1,000 loads × 0.80 pJ = 46,675.2 pJ − 800 pJ = <strong>45,875.2 pJ</strong>.</li></ul></li>
<li>Storage: 128 entries × 17 bits / 8 bits/byte = 272 bytes, 512 entries × 17 bits / 8 bits/byte = 1,088 bytes, and 2,048 entries × 17 bits / 8 bits/byte = 4,352 bytes.
<ul><li>128 to 512 entries: (41,601.6 pJ − 32,204.4 pJ) / (1,088 bytes − 272 bytes) = 9,397.2 pJ / 816 bytes = <strong>11.5 pJ per byte</strong> per 1,000 loads.</li><li>512 to 2,048 entries: (45,875.2 pJ − 41,601.6 pJ) / (4,352 bytes − 1,088 bytes) = 4,273.6 pJ / 3,264 bytes = <strong>1.31 pJ per byte</strong> per 1,000 loads.</li></ul>
<strong>512 entries is the knee</strong>: the second step returns about 9× less per byte. 2,048 entries still saves the most in total, so it wins only if the area is free and its lookup fits in the rename stages.</li></ol>
<p>The "hottest PCs that fit" assumption is the optimistic part. A real LRU table with a 2,048-PC footprint keeps fewer than the hottest 512, so the measured 512-entry result will sit below this line, and that gap is exactly what the replacement study measures.</p>`, "hard") +
M(`<p>A fully associative 512-entry bank prediction table starts empty. A server loop repeats this pattern every iteration:</p><ul><li>400 hot load PCs, each run once per iteration, always in the same order</li><li>then 150 load PCs that are never seen again</li></ul><p>Every lookup that finds its PC counts as a hit. Compare these policies in steady state:</p><ol><li>LRU: insert at MRU, and promote to MRU on a hit.</li><li>LIP: insert at the LRU position, and promote to MRU on a hit.</li><li>LRU with a second-sighting filter: a PC is allocated only the second time it misses, using a separate filter that always remembers every PC.</li></ol><p>For each, find the hits per iteration and the hit rate, and the smallest table that would let plain LRU hit on every hot PC.</p>`,
`<ol><li><strong>LRU:</strong> between two runs of a hot PC, the other 399 hot PCs and the 150 one-time PCs are looked up, which is 399 PCs + 150 PCs = 549 distinct PCs. LRU keeps a PC only if fewer than 512 distinct PCs come between its uses, so every hot PC is evicted first: <strong>0 hits</strong>, a 0% hit rate.</li><li><strong>LIP:</strong> in iteration 1, the 400 hot PCs fill the empty table, each inserted below the ones before it, then 112 one-time PCs fill the remaining 512 entries − 400 entries = 112 entries. The last 38 one-time PCs each evict the entry at the LRU position, which is the one-time PC inserted just before it. From iteration 2 on, every hot PC hits and moves to MRU, and every one-time PC replaces the previous one at the LRU position, so no hot PC is ever the victim: <strong>400 hits per iteration</strong>, a hit rate of 400 hits / (400 lookups + 150 lookups) = <strong>72.7%</strong>. The 111 one-time PCs from iteration 1 stay in the table forever, unused, because nothing ever evicts them.</li><li><strong>Second-sighting filter:</strong> hot PCs are allocated in iteration 2 and hit from iteration 3 on. One-time PCs are never seen twice, so they are never allocated, and the table holds just the 400 hot PCs: <strong>400 hits per iteration</strong>, also <strong>72.7%</strong>, with 112 entries left free.</li><li><strong>Smallest table for LRU:</strong> 549 other PCs plus the hot PC itself must fit, so <strong>550 entries</strong>, 7.4% more than 512, would take LRU from 0% to 72.7%.</li></ol>
<p>The LIP result shows why insertion matters more than eviction, and its stale entries show why LIP needs an occasional MRU insertion (BIP) to adapt when the hot set changes. The filter avoids the stale entries but costs its own storage. A size sweep with LRU alone would show a cliff between 549 and 550 entries, which says more about the policy than about the size.</p>`, "hard"),
}
].map(tabbed));
})();
